summary:
- topic: FY25 Budget Overview
  text: The Superintendent presented the Superintendent’sFY25Budget, which includes
    a 5% operating increase and a 3.8% school tax rate increase. He highlighted major
    constraints, including rising fixed costs and the loss of $1.5 million in federal
    COVID funds, which currently supports 19 positions, necessitating difficult decisions
    about district priorities.
- topic: Budget Adjustments and ELO Program
  text: 'The administration announced two key budget changes: an art teacher position,
    previously funded by COVID-era grants, will be moved into the local budget. To
    offset this, a one-year-only teaching position at Dyer School will be funded from
    an elementary school reserve account. The Superintendent also committed to continuing
    the valued Extended Learning Opportunities (ELO) program by finding an alternative
    staffing and funding solution after its state grant expires.'
- topic: Board Discussion on Budget Priorities
  text: Board members expressed strong support for continuing the ELO program and
    restoring the art position, sharing personal anecdotes about their importance.
    Members DeAngelis and Ryan advocated for exploring a dedicated grant writer position
    to secure funding for programs that cannot be included in the local budget. The
    board requested more details on the ELO transition plan before the final budget
    vote.
- topic: Special Education Department Presentation
  text: Director Kat Cox and her team provided an overview of the Instructional Support
    department, which serves 649 students with disabilities. They detailed the Child
    Find process, the development of Individualized Education Programs (IEPs), and
    the legal requirement to provide services in the least restrictive environment,
    emphasizing the collaborative, multi-disciplinary team approach to meeting individual
    student needs.
- topic: Adult Education Department Growth
  text: Director Erin Vicki highlighted the exponential growth of the Adult Education
    program, which is now the third largest in Maine, serving over 250 students primarily
    in English language acquisition. She noted that while the program has expanded
    its offerings, it remains heavily reliant on unstable grant funding, signaling
    a potential future need for increased local budget support to ensure sustainability.
- topic: Athletics Department Presentation
  text: Athletics Director Todd Livingston reviewed the department's 25 high school
    and 18 middle school programs, which involve over 40% of the student body. He
    highlighted the department's core covenants of respect, excellence, and tradition,
    and celebrated numerous team championships and individual student-athlete awards
    from the past year.
- topic: Operations & Safety Department Overview
  text: 'Director Mike Nally presented on the four departments under his purview:
    facilities, transportation, food service, and safety. He explained that significant
    budget increases in transportation and facilities are necessary to right-size
    previously underfunded areas, particularly for contracted bus services and rising
    utility costs. He also noted that the food service budget is projected to be balanced
    without a fund transfer for the first time in years.'
- topic: Public Comment
  text: Citizens spoke in strong support of the Special Education and Athletics departments,
    sharing personal stories of their positive impact. One parent, whose son is a
    graduate, read a letter from him detailing how the ELO program provided him with
    a clear career path in firefighting, urging the board to maintain the program
    and its dedicated coordinator.
